to understand my tendency

00:33:44.180 --> 00:33:46.777
and then put it in its place and say,

00:33:46.777 --> 00:33:48.687
"Hey, I still do have ambition.

00:33:48.687 --> 00:33:49.747
"I'm not just somebody

00:33:49.747 --> 00:33:54.110
"who just wants to maintain a lifestyle."

00:33:54.110 --> 00:33:56.560
I don't wanna be that person.

00:33:56.560 --> 00:34:01.560
But I think we have different,
different instinctive drives

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:05.180
at the heart of us.

00:34:05.180 --> 00:34:07.450
With me it's more of like, maintain.

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:10.390
Yeah and my desire to work on other things

00:34:10.390 --> 00:34:12.113
is not really tied to any,

00:34:13.349 --> 00:34:17.420
it's definitely not tied to
finances because I already know

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:19.660
that the most lucrative
thing that I'll ever do

00:34:19.660 --> 00:34:20.650
is Good Mythical Morning.

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:23.710
I mean very likely,
statistically speaking.

00:34:23.710 --> 00:34:25.218
Yeah.
Right?

00:34:25.218 --> 00:34:27.590
Because it's just so much content

00:34:27.590 --> 00:34:30.890
and it's just, it works
really well for us.

00:34:30.890 --> 00:34:34.850
If we, we would have to have
just like this total moonshot

00:34:34.850 --> 00:34:39.850
of a moment in, in TV or film or whatever.

00:34:40.565 --> 00:34:42.560
And again.
To begin to approach

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:44.900
the success.
I also, I wanna.

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:46.150
Because a lot of people,

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:47.610
whenever we talk like this,

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:48.443
a lot of people are like,

00:34:48.443 --> 00:34:50.577
"Guys, why do you, you have this obsession

00:34:50.577 --> 00:34:55.577
"with traditional media and
it's only because of your age.

00:34:55.677 --> 00:34:58.700
"And because you guys
grew up with TV and film."

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:00.700
Yeah, which to The Sopranos point.

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:02.570
Yeah and.
It's like why would you?

00:35:02.570 --> 00:35:06.206
You blazed the trail for
every other scripted show

00:35:06.206 --> 00:35:08.560
and you still set the standard.

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:10.830
People are rediscovering
your work every day

00:35:10.830 --> 00:35:12.330
and appreciating it.

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:14.440
Somehow because of TikTok.
Well.

00:35:14.440 --> 00:35:17.002
They're watching Sopranos.
And I think,

00:35:17.002 --> 00:35:20.894
and I think that my particular hangup

00:35:20.894 --> 00:35:23.666
is more, I'm saying this.

00:35:23.666 --> 00:35:26.590
I think my particular
hangup is more justified

00:35:26.590 --> 00:35:29.850
than The Sopranos guy's
hangup and here's why.

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:32.190
Because he got to create a world.

00:35:32.190 --> 00:35:34.240
The difference between
The Sopranos and a film,

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:35.370
again it's just.
Right.

00:35:35.370 --> 00:35:36.810
It's format.

00:35:36.810 --> 00:35:40.130
The difference between,
when I say TV and film.

00:35:40.130 --> 00:35:43.550
I don't mean, I don't care
about traditional media.

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:46.739
If you told me tomorrow that me and you

00:35:46.739 --> 00:35:48.490
could make a movie with an adequate budget

00:35:48.490 --> 00:35:50.460
in order to pull it off in
the way that we wanted to

00:35:50.460 --> 00:35:52.710
and it would just go straight to YouTube,

00:35:52.710 --> 00:35:54.744
I'd, that's fine.

00:35:54.744 --> 00:35:57.050
I'm not trying to win an Oscar.

00:35:57.050 --> 00:36:00.870
I'm not trying to get
respect from any particular

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:02.820
part of the entertainment industry.

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:06.060
I just want to be able to
tell the kinds of stories

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:07.033
that we wanna tell.

00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:11.380
And right now there's
just not a financial model

00:36:11.380 --> 00:36:14.330
to do that in the sort of completely owned

00:36:14.330 --> 00:36:16.900
and controlled independent
vehicle that we have right now

00:36:16.900 --> 00:36:18.060
with digital media.

00:36:18.060 --> 00:36:20.846
So it kind of goes
through the powers that be

00:36:20.846 --> 00:36:22.080
and the finances and all that stuff.

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:24.230
So it has nothing to do with an obsession

00:36:24.230 --> 00:36:27.730
with TV and film and traditional.

00:36:27.730 --> 00:36:31.740
It's more an obsession
with storytelling, now.

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:34.773
Until, until you check that box

00:36:34.773 --> 00:36:37.930
and then you see what it becomes next.

00:36:37.930 --> 00:36:40.960
For you, because it is a moving target.

00:36:40.960 --> 00:36:45.408
Well but it's something,
but it's the first thing.

00:36:45.408 --> 00:36:46.720
It's the first, I don't agree with that.

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:48.240
Okay.
Because yeah,

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:50.390
my interests are a moving target

00:36:50.390 --> 00:36:52.240
but we got into this business

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:54.320
because we wanted to tell stories.

00:36:54.320 --> 00:36:57.600
We wrote Gutless Wonders at 14

00:36:57.600 --> 00:36:59.080
because we wanted to tell stories.

00:36:59.080 --> 00:37:02.720
We did a blood oath because
we wanted to tell stories.

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:06.130
Like when we said we wanted
to create something together,

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:08.530
we didn't know what it was specifically

00:37:08.530 --> 00:37:11.190
but both of us were talking about,

00:37:11.190 --> 00:37:12.470
we were talking about
going to film school.

00:37:12.470 --> 00:37:14.340
Sure.
We were talking about

00:37:14.340 --> 00:37:17.290
creating a world, telling stories

00:37:17.290 --> 00:37:21.960
and we have been able to do
that in some fashion right?

00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:24.700
And we got to write a
novel that was a world

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:27.190
that came to life and maybe
that will become something.

00:37:27.190 --> 00:37:29.703
It isn't that we're not
trying, we're still trying.

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:33.290
But yeah, to me it's
the difference between

00:37:33.290 --> 00:37:35.770
what we do on Good
Mythical Morning which is,

00:37:35.770 --> 00:37:40.150
it has an incredible value
and is incredibly rewarding.

00:37:40.150 --> 00:37:42.870
It's just, the creative
process for me personally

00:37:42.870 --> 00:37:45.670
is a totally different thing
than something that we have

00:37:46.920 --> 00:37:49.190
conceptualized, written and executed

00:37:49.190 --> 00:37:50.170
and birthed in that way.

00:37:50.170 --> 00:37:51.003
I get that.

00:37:51.003 --> 00:37:53.363
And I think if we, if we shift to,

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:59.240
from a personal and
philosophical standpoint

00:37:59.240 --> 00:38:03.970
what it means to be, to
process being middle aged,

00:38:03.970 --> 00:38:08.320
I think we could
superimpose that discussion

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:10.200
over top of what we've already discussed

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:12.800
because the next thing
that comes to my mind

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:14.920
that I'm grateful for about my age

00:38:14.920 --> 00:38:18.853
is just starting to realize perspective.

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:26.530
Starting to realize
that I actually do have

00:38:26.610 --> 00:38:28.633
some accumulated wisdom.

00:38:30.073 --> 00:38:34.300
And for me, the first place my brain goes

00:38:34.300 --> 00:38:36.183
that I'm grateful for is,

00:38:37.170 --> 00:38:42.170
I feel like I'm well
positioned to know myself.

00:38:43.870 --> 00:38:45.410
Like I was telling Christy,

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:49.907
at a younger age, I feel like
I might have had the desire

00:38:49.907 --> 00:38:53.740
and some of the drive to know myself,

00:38:53.740 --> 00:38:58.160
understand what makes me
tick and what ticks me off

00:38:58.160 --> 00:39:00.130
and every other type of tick.

00:39:00.130 --> 00:39:03.570
But there's something
about this age that I can,

00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:07.890
I can start to really experience
a level of self knowledge

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:12.290
that I just don't think was
possible when we were in college

00:39:12.290 --> 00:39:15.140
or when we were grinding

00:39:15.140 --> 00:39:18.520
and trying to make it early on you know?

00:39:18.520 --> 00:39:20.610
So I'm actually enjoying that.

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:24.210
That, again we have the luxury of having

00:39:24.210 --> 00:39:29.210
some space and time to
give attention to ourselves

00:39:29.790 --> 00:39:33.220
in a way that's like, I'm still evolving.

00:39:33.220 --> 00:39:36.350
I'm still changing and
I'm very committed to

00:39:36.350 --> 00:39:38.350
being in touch with that process

00:39:38.350 --> 00:39:40.653
and understanding myself.

00:39:40.653 --> 00:39:41.740
And I think that that is,

00:39:41.740 --> 00:39:44.600
a portion of that with us is aging.

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:47.320
I think also a big portion of it is

00:39:47.320 --> 00:39:49.750
the worldview change that we had right?

00:39:49.750 --> 00:39:51.390
So I think.
Yeah it's like,

00:39:51.390 --> 00:39:53.730
we were being redefined in

00:39:53.730 --> 00:39:55.610
our late 30s.
But specifically,

00:39:55.610 --> 00:39:57.790
what I'm getting at is,

00:39:57.790 --> 00:40:00.110
when you are an evangelical Christian

00:40:00.110 --> 00:40:03.220
or when I was an evangelical Christian,

00:40:03.220 --> 00:40:08.220
there is a, thinking a little bit too,

00:40:08.230 --> 00:40:10.073
too much focus on self.

00:40:11.791 --> 00:40:14.570
It's all about attention to God right

00:40:14.570 --> 00:40:16.260
and you are small and God is big

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:19.130
and there are some very good
things about a mentality

00:40:19.130 --> 00:40:22.670
of not centering yourself
and humbling yourself

00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:25.060
and I believe that there's
some good qualities there.

00:40:25.060 --> 00:40:27.810
But one of the things that
kind of gets wrapped up in that

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:29.950
is that any sort of self discovery

00:40:29.950 --> 00:40:31.650
and looking in to yourself

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:33.310
and knowing that there's
something in there

00:40:33.310 --> 00:40:35.340
that needs to be
expressed and to come out.

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:39.660
Could begin, you start
confusing that with sinful pride

00:40:39.660 --> 00:40:42.910
and so the way my personality processed

00:40:42.910 --> 00:40:46.500
a lot of that structure
was this feeling that like,

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:50.580
I'm not important enough to
really figure myself out.

00:40:50.580 --> 00:40:52.050
Now I not saying that's everybody

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:54.340
but I think it stunted my growth

00:40:54.340 --> 00:40:57.470
of self realization and self discovery.

00:40:57.470 --> 00:41:00.815
When I got out of that way
of thinking about things

00:41:00.815 --> 00:41:02.610
and started thinking like,

00:41:02.610 --> 00:41:05.210
I gotta really figure myself out.

00:41:05.210 --> 00:41:08.690
It's not just about understanding God

00:41:08.690 --> 00:41:13.090
and the Bible and then my,
my relationship to that,

00:41:13.090 --> 00:41:16.670
but it's also like this is
the body and mind that I have.

00:41:16.670 --> 00:41:19.700
I need to figure out who I am

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:22.959
and there's nothing wrong
with that and that sort of,

00:41:22.959 --> 00:41:25.990
you can call it selfishness

00:41:25.990 --> 00:41:29.950
but directing some actual
attention to yourself,

00:41:29.950 --> 00:41:32.310
learning how to love yourself

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:35.760
so that you can actually be a
better citizen and a better,

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:39.870
love people better and be less
selfish and self absorbed.

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:43.410
For me, I think that was
the beginning of a process

00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:47.330
that eventually led to
therapy and led to the sort of

00:41:47.330 --> 00:41:49.710
self discovery and figuring out who I am

00:41:49.710 --> 00:41:51.170
and as a Enneagram 3,

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:54.440
another thing that happens
is that you're really good

00:41:54.440 --> 00:41:57.510
at figuring out who everyone
else wants you to be.

00:41:57.510 --> 00:42:01.600
You can analyze a person
or a group of people,

00:42:01.600 --> 00:42:06.220
figure out the ideal and then
you just adopt that ideal.

00:42:10.250 --> 00:42:12.783
And it wasn't until the
past five years or so

00:42:12.783 --> 00:42:13.830
that I began to figure out

00:42:13.830 --> 00:42:17.890
that so many of the things that I liked

00:42:17.890 --> 00:42:20.830
or a lot of the ways
that I presented myself

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:23.840
were just based on people's perception

00:42:23.840 --> 00:42:25.560
and so for me, getting older, a part of it

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:28.273
has been A, discovering myself.

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:33.270
But B, also being able to
embrace some things about myself

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:36.770
irrespective of people's
perspective of me.

00:42:36.770 --> 00:42:39.260
Is there an example that comes to mind

00:42:39.260 --> 00:42:41.360
of something that you
realize you were doing

00:42:41.360 --> 00:42:42.313
because of that?

00:42:43.230 --> 00:42:46.064
Because I don't know exactly
what you're talking about

00:42:46.064 --> 00:42:48.260
as it relates to you.

00:42:48.260 --> 00:42:53.260
Well it's more of a disposition
of thinking about like,

00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:59.727
oh is that something that I want

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:02.100
or is that something that I think

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:03.893
that someone thinks I should want?

00:43:05.290 --> 00:43:07.510
And it could be something as simple as

00:43:07.510 --> 00:43:12.470
oh I'm going to like
this movie or whatever,

00:43:12.470 --> 00:43:14.870
I'm going to say that I like this movie

00:43:14.870 --> 00:43:17.770
because I think that other
people like this movie

00:43:17.770 --> 00:43:19.330
and I started realizing that

00:43:19.330 --> 00:43:21.940
it was just kind of built into my DNA

00:43:21.940 --> 00:43:26.940
to be able to anticipate what
the collective consciousness

00:43:27.320 --> 00:43:29.770
about something was gonna
be and tapping into that

00:43:29.770 --> 00:43:31.970
and this is still a struggle for me.

00:43:31.970 --> 00:43:36.940
To be like what do you
actually think about this?

00:43:36.940 --> 00:43:39.611
Right, I mean a good example
is what we talked about

00:43:39.611 --> 00:43:43.303
a couple weeks ago with the guitar right?

00:43:44.302 --> 00:43:46.680
Oh.
Getting a Taylor guitar

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:50.697
because it was what I thought
that I should like at the time

00:43:50.697 --> 00:43:52.460
and environment that I was in.

00:43:52.460 --> 00:43:55.260
And then slowly as I got older,

00:43:55.260 --> 00:43:58.747
being like I find myself
liking these other sounds

00:43:58.747 --> 00:44:01.230
and then I go and I look up well,

00:44:01.230 --> 00:44:02.280
what does so and so play?

00:44:02.280 --> 00:44:03.113
What does so and so play?

00:44:03.113 --> 00:44:06.000
That's not a Taylor, that's a Martin.

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:07.350
Okay.
Or that's a Gibson.

00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:09.300
And it's like that's a sound
that you actually liked

00:44:09.300 --> 00:44:12.110
but when I went to a music store,

00:44:12.110 --> 00:44:14.170
even the last time I bought a guitar,

00:44:14.170 --> 00:44:16.320
when I went into the music
store it's like I only,

00:44:16.320 --> 00:44:18.900
I sat down and I just
played the Taylors right?

00:44:18.900 --> 00:44:20.660
And it's just this weird thing

00:44:20.660 --> 00:44:21.590
and it's not because that I think

00:44:21.590 --> 00:44:23.160
oh liking Taylor is better.

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:25.900
It's just you made that
decision in the past

00:44:25.900 --> 00:44:28.360
for somebody else and now you
kind of just carry it through

00:44:28.360 --> 00:44:29.513
into adulthood.

00:44:30.350 --> 00:44:33.100
So it's kind of this peeling
back things like that.

00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:36.290
Yeah the peeling back experience

00:44:36.290 --> 00:44:38.680
to get to the core of myself is something

00:44:38.680 --> 00:44:41.870
that I'm now in a position to do.

00:44:41.870 --> 00:44:46.807
For me, Evangelicalism,
growing up in that was like,

00:44:46.807 --> 00:44:51.050
"Okay, I want to
aggressively learn the system

00:44:51.050 --> 00:44:54.457
"so that then I can know enough about it

00:44:54.457 --> 00:44:58.767
"and put into practice
the spiritual disciplines

00:44:58.767 --> 00:45:02.459
"and then I can go about
the rest of my life

00:45:02.459 --> 00:45:05.887
"having fully explored the manual

00:45:05.887 --> 00:45:07.210
"and just executing it."

00:45:07.210 --> 00:45:09.600
Yeah.
It's not about asking

00:45:09.600 --> 00:45:13.650
difficult questions or
wringing my soul out

00:45:13.650 --> 00:45:18.650
to really analyze who I am
and the whys of life for me.

00:45:20.723 --> 00:45:24.930
Because that felt like a scary burden

00:45:24.930 --> 00:45:27.180
apart from someone just
giving me all the answers

00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:29.200
in a nice package.

00:45:29.200 --> 00:45:30.033
Yeah.
Turns out

00:45:30.033 --> 00:45:32.810
the package wasn't completely nice

00:45:32.810 --> 00:45:35.100
but I gained a lot of security from that

00:45:35.100 --> 00:45:37.850
and I did have this mentality that was,

00:45:37.850 --> 00:45:42.130
I'm going to just, the
second half of my life

00:45:42.130 --> 00:45:45.340
is just gonna be about applying everything

00:45:45.340 --> 00:45:47.130
that I've already learned.

00:45:47.130 --> 00:45:49.070
I just didn't anticipate

00:45:49.070 --> 00:45:51.920
that there could be so much
more to learn out there

00:45:51.920 --> 00:45:54.450
and there was a lot of fear

00:45:54.450 --> 00:45:56.530
in crossing over to the other side

00:45:56.530 --> 00:46:00.760
because of all the unknowns
that we've discussed

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:03.500
but yeah, actually being
there is a very freeing

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:07.630
and rewarding, and still
at times very scary process

00:46:07.630 --> 00:46:12.630
because it's, it's not
well laid out and rigid.

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:17.500
It's much more fluid.

00:46:17.500 --> 00:46:22.070
But it's enabled me to know myself

00:46:22.070 --> 00:46:23.270
at a totally deeper level

00:46:23.270 --> 00:46:26.690
and I think that's the experience

00:46:26.690 --> 00:46:28.853
that I want to define my life.

00:46:29.939 --> 00:46:32.140
I found myself talking a lot in therapy

00:46:32.140 --> 00:46:36.030
about situations that I'm
going through and experiencing

00:46:36.030 --> 00:46:39.773
and then discussing making meaning of it.

00:46:41.209 --> 00:46:44.220
You know, how as humans we
want to, we wanna make meaning

00:46:44.220 --> 00:46:46.600
and that enriches an experience

00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:51.550
and kind of codifies it in our memories.

00:46:51.550 --> 00:46:56.550
As not, and being present and
making the most of something.

00:46:58.280 --> 00:47:01.270
Just wringing everything
out of it that there is

00:47:01.270 --> 00:47:04.110
I think is the philosophy

00:47:04.110 --> 00:47:06.550
and the approach that I'm taking now.

00:47:06.550 --> 00:47:07.750
And it's something that,

00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:10.955
I think it's still a struggle for me.

00:47:10.955 --> 00:47:12.310
It's something I've identified,

00:47:12.310 --> 00:47:14.530
this is how I wanna live my life.

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:16.840
But I also know enough about myself that

00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:20.317
I'm prone to be stuck in my ways.

00:47:20.317 --> 00:47:23.440
Like you talk about the
second, the second half of life

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:27.630
and just the whole trope
of being set in my ways

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:29.470
is something that is a discussion

00:47:29.470 --> 00:47:31.720
that goes on a lot at my house.

00:47:31.720 --> 00:47:36.240
Whether it's you know, just
deciding that I want things

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:39.850
to be in a certain way and
routines are so huge for me.

00:47:39.850 --> 00:47:42.542
Especially as an Enneagram 1.

00:47:42.542 --> 00:47:43.910
It's like once I've figured something out,

00:47:43.910 --> 00:47:46.970
I'm gonna make this movie
this way and not only that

00:47:46.970 --> 00:47:49.490
but I'm gonna make it at this time

00:47:49.490 --> 00:47:51.904
and I'm gonna get my
ingredients in this way

00:47:51.904 --> 00:47:56.200
and if anybody messes anything
up, that really irks me.

00:47:56.200 --> 00:47:59.210
Or I have these plans to
do something with the kids.

00:47:59.210 --> 00:48:02.020
Like yesterday, we're
gonna go one wheeling

00:48:02.020 --> 00:48:03.570
and I'm texting you

00:48:03.570 --> 00:48:05.280
so I can borrow one for you from Lincoln.

00:48:05.280 --> 00:48:07.210
I'm like, "Make sure
thing thing's charged up."

00:48:07.210 --> 00:48:11.460
And then I go out to get them, mine,

00:48:11.460 --> 00:48:13.170
and put them in the
trunk before I go pick up

00:48:13.170 --> 00:48:15.430
your fully charged one when I realize,

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:17.720
Lando's has been in the
back of my car for two weeks

00:48:17.720 --> 00:48:21.773
and it barely has any
charge and I was so upset.

00:48:22.850 --> 00:48:24.810
And that's one of the first things

00:48:24.810 --> 00:48:28.190
when I was bouncing this
discussion off of Christy,

00:48:28.190 --> 00:48:30.737
she was like, you know,

00:48:30.737 --> 00:48:33.487
"Getting older, you have a tendency

00:48:33.487 --> 00:48:36.747
"to be set in your ways and
anything that throws that off,

00:48:36.747 --> 00:48:41.047
"just it really, it really throws you off.

00:48:41.047 --> 00:48:43.850
"And if you don't."

00:48:43.850 --> 00:48:44.720
And I agree with this,

00:48:44.720 --> 00:48:46.840
so it wasn't like a warning out the blue.

00:48:46.840 --> 00:48:48.116
Right.
= But it was like,

00:48:48.116 --> 00:48:49.317
"Hey if you don't look out for this,

00:48:49.317 --> 00:48:52.027
"you're gonna become
somebody who builds this,

00:48:52.027 --> 00:48:55.007
"these walls around yourself and says,

00:48:55.007 --> 00:48:56.797
"'I'm just trying to maintain what I have

00:48:56.797 --> 00:48:59.390
"'and the way that I want it.'"

00:48:59.390 --> 00:49:03.780
And then it lets fewer and
fewer new experiences in.

00:49:03.780 --> 00:49:06.530
So I think that's the, that's the tension

00:49:06.530 --> 00:49:10.280
that I'm experiencing at
this point in midlife is

00:49:11.210 --> 00:49:14.890
knowing enough of who I am
to celebrate and love myself

00:49:14.890 --> 00:49:18.260
but not allow that to keep me from

00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:20.160
who I'm still going to become.

00:49:20.160 --> 00:49:22.590
Yeah.
So even having

00:49:22.590 --> 00:49:24.484
the words to say that

00:49:24.484 --> 00:49:28.200
is a part of the journey that I've been on

00:49:28.200 --> 00:49:29.860
just in the last few years

00:49:29.860 --> 00:49:33.920
and that I really enjoy in saying,

00:49:33.920 --> 00:49:36.450
you know, the openness
from leaving Evangelicalism

00:49:36.450 --> 00:49:40.320
and saying, "I wanna be open
minded and open hearted."

00:49:40.320 --> 00:49:42.360
And then applying that to things beyond

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:43.690
just your spiritual beliefs

00:49:43.690 --> 00:49:45.760
but to every aspect of your life

00:49:45.760 --> 00:49:49.580
is something that I'm still growing into.

00:49:49.580 --> 00:49:54.400
Well one of the things
that maybe an unspoken,

00:49:54.400 --> 00:49:55.980
it's actually not unspoken.

00:49:55.980 --> 00:50:00.930
Because Evangelical Christians
and Christians in general

00:50:00.930 --> 00:50:03.680
see God, one of the
characteristics of God,

00:50:03.680 --> 00:50:06.723
the attributes of God is
His immutability right?

00:50:07.830 --> 00:50:08.663
Which means.
Doesn't change.

00:50:08.663 --> 00:50:10.160
He does not change.

00:50:10.160 --> 00:50:11.440
That's one of the things
that makes God God.

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:13.480
He is always the same.

00:50:13.480 --> 00:50:17.700
So that becomes something
that you kind of aspire to.

00:50:17.700 --> 00:50:19.420
Implicitly.

00:50:19.420 --> 00:50:21.320
And I think there's also an obsession

00:50:21.320 --> 00:50:22.290
in our culture.
'Cause when you're right,

00:50:22.290 --> 00:50:23.123
why change?

00:50:23.123 --> 00:50:25.840
There's also an obsession in our culture

00:50:25.840 --> 00:50:29.100
with staying the same right?

00:50:29.100 --> 00:50:33.730
We get so mad when a politician
flip flops on something.

00:50:33.730 --> 00:50:36.270
And it's like, that's
the stupidest perspective

00:50:36.270 --> 00:50:39.160
I've ever heard in my life
because you know what?

00:50:39.160 --> 00:50:43.120
New information causes
people to change their mind.

00:50:43.120 --> 00:50:44.810
Now it's one thing if somebody flip flops,

00:50:44.810 --> 00:50:48.040
and many politicians flip
flop because of convenience

00:50:48.040 --> 00:50:51.450
and wanting to be more electable.

00:50:51.450 --> 00:50:52.640
That's one thing.
Right.

00:50:52.640 --> 00:50:53.557
But when somebody's like,

00:50:53.557 --> 00:50:55.607
"I used to think this about this issue,

00:50:55.607 --> 00:50:58.830
"now I think this because
my view has evolved."

00:50:58.830 --> 00:51:00.250
That's something that should be,

00:51:00.250 --> 00:51:02.020
that process should be celebrated.

00:51:02.020 --> 00:51:04.350
That's not some reason
not to like somebody.

00:51:04.350 --> 00:51:05.670
But I think one of the things that

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:09.320
because we have experienced
this incredible evolution

00:51:09.320 --> 00:51:11.745
and this upheaval of our worldview,

00:51:11.745 --> 00:51:16.745
it's given us this, just
sort of underlying belief

00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:21.300
that it's not about being this certain way

00:51:21.300 --> 00:51:23.080
and figuring that out and
just staying that way.

00:51:23.080 --> 00:51:26.190
It's just like, there's a version
of Rhett 10 years from now

00:51:26.190 --> 00:51:27.857
and then a version of
Rhett 20 years from now

00:51:27.857 --> 00:51:31.040
and I hope that those versions are better

00:51:31.040 --> 00:51:34.420
but I know that those versions
are going to be different

00:51:34.420 --> 00:51:37.840
and they may be different in
ways that I can't anticipate

00:51:37.840 --> 00:51:40.420
and that's a super exciting thing.

00:51:40.420 --> 00:51:42.150
Now there are some things that may be

00:51:42.150 --> 00:51:44.880
more of a refining process of
kind of figuring yourself out.

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:46.580
You know, when you talk about the,

00:51:47.500 --> 00:51:49.870
the sort of savoring
and being in the moment.

00:51:49.870 --> 00:51:53.310
One of the things that I've
kind of embraced about myself

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:57.690
is that I have a really
difficult time doing that right?

00:51:57.690 --> 00:52:00.980
We even wrote on our whole chapter

00:52:00.980 --> 00:52:03.990
in the Book of Mythicality
about stopping and celebrating

00:52:03.990 --> 00:52:08.170
and I'm not saying that I don't
agree with that in general.

00:52:08.170 --> 00:52:13.170
If you achieve something,
you know, stop and enjoy it.

00:52:14.190 --> 00:52:16.370
And really appreciate this moment.

00:52:16.370 --> 00:52:18.260
But there's also just a,

00:52:18.260 --> 00:52:20.595
there's the way that I am

00:52:20.595 --> 00:52:21.730
and the way that I sort
of operate in the world

00:52:21.730 --> 00:52:25.550
is that the moment that
I achieve one goal,

00:52:25.550 --> 00:52:28.350
I'm ready for the next
thing and I think that,

00:52:28.350 --> 00:52:29.440
I'm starting to understand

00:52:29.440 --> 00:52:32.400
that that's not a bad thing for me.

00:52:32.400 --> 00:52:34.060
You know what I'm saying?

00:52:34.060 --> 00:52:36.720
That whole process,
like every single stage

00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:40.530
along the different steps of a project

00:52:40.530 --> 00:52:42.960
and then it finally coming to fruition,

00:52:42.960 --> 00:52:46.530
when I get kind of focused
on that moment of achieving

00:52:46.530 --> 00:52:48.297
and that moment of
completing and being like,

00:52:48.297 --> 00:52:50.797
"Hold on everybody, let's
stop and really celebrate

00:52:50.797 --> 00:52:53.357
"and let's go to dinner
and let's really milk this

00:52:53.357 --> 00:52:55.000
"for all it's worth."

00:52:55.000 --> 00:52:57.260
For me it's kind of like,

00:52:57.260 --> 00:52:59.820
I wanna milk each step for all it's worth

00:52:59.820 --> 00:53:02.720
and make sure that I'm
not just looking at that,

00:53:02.720 --> 00:53:05.090
accomplishing the goal
as being the end all

00:53:05.090 --> 00:53:08.100
because really, it may be, like I said,

00:53:08.100 --> 00:53:10.510
the writing, the
conceptualizing that ends up,

00:53:10.510 --> 00:53:11.660
when I look back on this,

00:53:11.660 --> 00:53:13.580
the most fun part of this whole process

00:53:13.580 --> 00:53:16.260
wasn't when we finally
screened that movie,

00:53:16.260 --> 00:53:18.080
it was when we came up with the idea

00:53:18.080 --> 00:53:19.800
or when we had that breakthrough

00:53:19.800 --> 00:53:21.800
in the storytelling process.

00:53:21.800 --> 00:53:23.430
Then the thing is is that,

00:53:23.430 --> 00:53:25.590
it's not gonna make me any happier anyway

00:53:25.590 --> 00:53:27.290
so why don't you just
move on to the next thing?

00:53:27.290 --> 00:53:31.637
Like I'm learning to not
get upset with myself for,

00:53:34.520 --> 00:53:36.070
I don't wanna say not being present

00:53:36.070 --> 00:53:37.150
because I wanna be present.

00:53:37.150 --> 00:53:38.950
I wanna be present at each step

00:53:38.950 --> 00:53:41.077
but not stopping and saying that like,

00:53:41.077 --> 00:53:45.120
"I'm going to make a huge
fricking deal out of this thing."

00:53:45.120 --> 00:53:48.012
It's like what, does
that come natural to me?

00:53:48.012 --> 00:53:50.300
Not really, I mean I'm gonna enjoy it

00:53:50.300 --> 00:53:52.670
and let's celebrate but hey,

00:53:52.670 --> 00:53:54.800
I know that the next day I'm gonna wake up

00:53:54.800 --> 00:53:55.930
and I'm gonna be like, "What's next?"

00:53:55.930 --> 00:53:57.107
And that's okay.

00:53:57.107 --> 00:53:59.590
And that may be something
that's good about me right?

00:53:59.590 --> 00:54:00.957
Versus being like,

00:54:00.957 --> 00:54:04.470
"Oh you can never be
satisfied and that's bad."

00:54:04.470 --> 00:54:09.070
Maybe that sort of insatiable
hunger for the next thing

00:54:09.070 --> 00:54:11.290
is just part of my
disposition and my personality

00:54:11.290 --> 00:54:13.150
and something I should embrace.

00:54:13.150 --> 00:54:14.520
The moment that I began thinking

00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:17.650
that I can find my happiness
in the things that I achieve,

00:54:17.650 --> 00:54:19.060
I've gone off track.

00:54:19.060 --> 00:54:23.330
But if I can find happiness in the process

00:54:23.330 --> 00:54:24.480
and knowing that hey man,

00:54:24.480 --> 00:54:25.453
oh there's one of the things
I've been talking about

00:54:25.453 --> 00:54:29.400
with my therapist is that as I talk about

00:54:29.400 --> 00:54:32.450
the different facets of
what we do professionally

00:54:32.450 --> 00:54:36.290
and of course being essentially CEOs

00:54:36.290 --> 00:54:40.119
of a media company takes
up a fair amount of time.

00:54:40.119 --> 00:54:43.871
And of course that's not my
favorite part of what we do.

00:54:43.871 --> 00:54:46.510
My favorite of what we
do is the creative part

00:54:46.510 --> 00:54:47.680
and the way he kind of,

00:54:47.680 --> 00:54:49.250
the way my therapist kind of described it,

00:54:49.250 --> 00:54:51.137
he was like, "You kind of get into,

00:54:51.137 --> 00:54:52.117
"the way you talk about it

00:54:52.117 --> 00:54:54.007
"is as if you're in sort of a zone

00:54:54.007 --> 00:54:55.697
"when you're in this creative zone

00:54:55.697 --> 00:54:58.787
"and as you get older
you're learning to make sure

00:54:58.787 --> 00:55:00.247
"that you're getting that time

00:55:00.247 --> 00:55:02.007
"and you're getting that creative space

00:55:02.007 --> 00:55:04.187
"because that's where you find life."

00:55:05.050 --> 00:55:08.005
And he's helped me be able to see that

00:55:08.005 --> 00:55:10.930
that's not an unhealthy thing

00:55:10.930 --> 00:55:13.160
because when I start describing it to him,

00:55:13.160 --> 00:55:15.850
I start describing it in this like,

00:55:15.850 --> 00:55:17.070
I feel a little bit guilty

00:55:17.070 --> 00:55:19.983
for how bad I wanna do X, Y and Z.

00:55:19.983 --> 00:55:20.970
That's my threeness.

00:55:20.970 --> 00:55:23.730
That's my achiever and that's my performer

00:55:23.730 --> 00:55:27.370
and that's bad because I'm
trying to find my identity

00:55:27.370 --> 00:55:28.370
in the things that I'm creating.

00:55:28.370 --> 00:55:29.695
And he's like,

00:55:29.695 --> 00:55:30.528
"Whoa whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:55:30.528 --> 00:55:31.361
"No, no no.

00:55:31.361 --> 00:55:35.877
"There is an incredible
goodness and health and life

00:55:37.537 --> 00:55:40.277
"in your being in a creative zone

00:55:40.277 --> 00:55:43.280
"and finding life in
the creative process."

00:55:43.280 --> 00:55:44.113
That's a good thing.

00:55:44.113 --> 00:55:45.190
Now the moment that you start trying

00:55:45.190 --> 00:55:46.530
to do it for other people

00:55:46.530 --> 00:55:48.950
and you're starting to do it for adulation

00:55:48.950 --> 00:55:51.280
and people to kind of pat you on the back,

00:55:51.280 --> 00:55:55.590
that's trouble but
understanding that you need that

00:55:55.590 --> 00:55:57.840
and you want that and you're never gonna,

00:55:57.840 --> 00:56:00.970
you're never gonna be satisfied
unless you're creating

00:56:00.970 --> 00:56:03.990
and that might be the next
thing, that's a good thing.

00:56:03.990 --> 00:56:05.520
So that's something that I've.

00:56:05.520 --> 00:56:08.680
So I've sort of let go of
my guilt of being like,

00:56:08.680 --> 00:56:09.820
what we talked about earlier.

00:56:09.820 --> 00:56:13.020
Like the fact that yeah we're
doing all this other stuff

00:56:13.020 --> 00:56:17.770
but I still have 10 other
things that I want us to do.

00:56:17.770 --> 00:56:19.857
That's not a bad thing
and I can embrace that

00:56:19.857 --> 00:56:21.223
and that's not unhealthy.

00:56:22.083 --> 00:56:23.780
You know what I'm saying?

00:56:23.780 --> 00:56:27.340
Yeah definitely and I think that it's,

00:56:27.340 --> 00:56:31.810
again it's back to understanding
enough about myself,

00:56:31.810 --> 00:56:35.257
you understanding enough
about yourself to,

00:56:35.257 --> 00:56:40.257
to begin to parse what
your motivations are

00:56:41.530 --> 00:56:43.520
but not a beat yourself up

00:56:43.520 --> 00:56:45.913
and not to over-interpret things.

00:56:45.913 --> 00:56:50.020
It's like every single thing
that you pour yourself into,

00:56:50.020 --> 00:56:53.420
there's, it could lead to a problem

00:56:53.420 --> 00:56:56.735
or it could lead to
something worth celebrating.

00:56:56.735 --> 00:56:59.410
It's great right?

00:56:59.410 --> 00:57:01.640
But if you're, yeah I'm glad to hear

00:57:01.640 --> 00:57:03.660
that you're not beating yourself up

00:57:03.660 --> 00:57:07.880
and just reducing your
desire to something that

00:57:09.046 --> 00:57:14.046
is motivated by the wrong
things but gaining clarity.

00:57:14.130 --> 00:57:16.420
I think as, again at our age,

00:57:16.420 --> 00:57:19.760
we can have enough
perspective to gain clarity

00:57:19.760 --> 00:57:23.530
on what a vibrant life can look

00:57:23.530 --> 00:57:25.380
and feel like for each of us.

00:57:25.380 --> 00:57:27.300
And understanding the parts of it

00:57:27.300 --> 00:57:29.220
that are actually healthy.

00:57:29.220 --> 00:57:30.933
That was a big shift for me.

00:57:33.670 --> 00:57:35.410
You know, I'm thinking about it

00:57:35.410 --> 00:57:38.820
because of this movie that
we're right in the middle of

00:57:38.820 --> 00:57:40.290
this creative process.

00:57:40.290 --> 00:57:43.820
If you told me, like I should be okay

00:57:43.820 --> 00:57:45.760
if for some reason I was
able to look in the future

00:57:45.760 --> 00:57:47.860
and find that this movie
was never going to be made

00:57:47.860 --> 00:57:50.370
into anything other than a script.

00:57:50.370 --> 00:57:51.960
Now that would be disheartening for sure.

00:57:51.960 --> 00:57:54.530
I like to believe that
it's going to happen

00:57:54.530 --> 00:57:59.530
but the process of writing
it should be enjoyable

00:58:02.130 --> 00:58:06.010
and vibrant and life-giving
regardless of the outcome.

00:58:06.010 --> 00:58:08.040
If it doesn't get made into a movie,

00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:12.020
it doesn't mean that the
process of writing it

00:58:12.020 --> 00:58:14.490
and developing it was for nothing

00:58:14.490 --> 00:58:17.740
because there was joy
and life in the process.

00:58:17.740 --> 00:58:19.162
Yeah.

00:58:19.162 --> 00:58:22.383
And that's a huge shift
for me as an achiever.

00:58:23.260 --> 00:58:26.770
I can't get rid of the engine
that is always putting me,

00:58:26.770 --> 00:58:29.550
is always in a certain gear and as soon,

00:58:29.550 --> 00:58:30.647
the moment you turn it off and say,

00:58:30.647 --> 00:58:32.090
"Hey let's the engine rest."

00:58:32.090 --> 00:58:34.690
I'm like, ah let's turn
the engine back on.

00:58:34.690 --> 00:58:36.300
I can't stop that about myself

00:58:36.300 --> 00:58:38.130
and that's not necessarily unhealthy.

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:40.590
It's the perspective if all of a sudden

00:58:40.590 --> 00:58:43.440
that engine is running for
everybody else's expectations.

00:58:44.320 --> 00:58:46.810
And so that's been a huge shift

00:58:46.810 --> 00:58:50.190
that's kind of happened in
the therapy process with me.

00:58:50.190 --> 00:58:52.520
Of kind of just eh, this is who you are.

00:58:52.520 --> 00:58:54.840
This is how you're built, embrace it

00:58:54.840 --> 00:58:55.980
and know the pitfalls.

00:58:55.980 --> 00:58:59.170
Yeah I mean there's so
many ways that we're alike,

00:58:59.170 --> 00:59:00.650
so many ways that we're different.

00:59:00.650 --> 00:59:01.610
When you talk about letting go

00:59:01.610 --> 00:59:04.470
of other people's
expectations for me, I'm like,

00:59:04.470 --> 00:59:07.100
yeah I think my practice

00:59:07.100 --> 00:59:09.270
is letting go of my own expectations.

00:59:09.270 --> 00:59:10.700
That I've created.
Yeah.

00:59:10.700 --> 00:59:13.400
And this you know, to have this sense of

00:59:14.490 --> 00:59:16.800
and practice of control.

00:59:16.800 --> 00:59:21.250
Like this morning I've got my
mountain bike ride that I do

00:59:21.250 --> 00:59:22.300
and it's the same.

00:59:22.300 --> 00:59:25.380
So I can measure myself against
it in order to get better,

00:59:25.380 --> 00:59:30.273
to be a little faster and more efficient.

00:59:30.273 --> 00:59:32.980
Which shows progress.

00:59:32.980 --> 00:59:36.900
But I heard an owl hooting
and I made a decision.

00:59:36.900 --> 00:59:39.350
You know what, I'm gonna find this out

00:59:39.350 --> 00:59:41.740
and this morning, you know
you've been talking about owls

00:59:41.740 --> 00:59:43.660
for a couple of months now
and how you've seen them.

00:59:43.660 --> 00:59:45.350
It's kind of really upset.
I've seen owls.

00:59:45.350 --> 00:59:46.970
I've not seen an owl.
Everywhere.

